    {
      "id": "FP-10",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "FirstPrinciple",
      "label": "The Internal Scorecard Is the Valid Authority for Self-Assessment",
      "text": "External validation optimizes for approval, not truth. Using crowd approval as a truth-signal hands your epistemology to whoever controls the crowd.",
      "notes": "Epictetus: 'When you look outside, you have handed over your autonomy.' LIMITATION (v1.9): FP-10 establishes autonomy from external judgment — what the scorecard measures against. It does not yet name the integration requirement: that the scorecard must itself be coherent across beliefs, stated principles, and concrete actions. The fuller test: not only 'did I consult myself?' but 'does my self-consultation reveal consistency?' See SA-6 (outcome attachment) and FR-19 (Negative Capability) for active calibration. STRUCTURAL NOTE: FP-10 currently grounds only DB-13. Under-deployed relative to its stated importance."
    },
    {
      "id": "FP-11",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "FirstPrinciple",
      "label": "Fear Is a Distortion Variable, Not a Reliable Signal",
      "text": "Fear of exclusion, loss, mortality, meaninglessness — a systemic distortion amplifier. Fear-driven arguments deserve structural skepticism. Fear is not a character flaw; it is a force to be managed.",
      "notes": "v2.0 amendment: Two categories of fear must be distinguished. (1) Naturally-arising fear: the organic response to real stakes — status loss, exclusion, identity threat. This was the original FP-11 scope. (2) Manufactured fear: fear that is *designed* by algorithmic architecture, attention-capture systems, and status-insecurity amplification — not incidental to the information system but a deliberate output of it. The manufactured category is strategically amplified, not merely exploited. This distinction matters for moral allocation: individuals experiencing manufactured fear are more constrained than individuals experiencing natural fear; the architects of manufactured fear bear the primary moral weight (see FR-14, and new FR-22)."
    },

