    {
      "id": "FP-12",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "FirstPrinciple",
      "label": "All Conscious Beings Deserve Respect and Certain Rights",
      "text": "Not limited to humans. Not limited to one country. All sentient, conscious beings have a claim to basic respect and certain rights. Complicated in practice — competing interests, capacity questions, implementation difficulties — but the principle is firm.",
      "notes": "Retired BS-6. Grounds: FP-5 (Singer's logic is species-neutral), FP-4, Thomas's behavior (no meat). Source: Thomas review 2026-03-06."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-14",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Distributed Moral Responsibility",
      "text": "When an actor is constrained into a choice between bad options, moral burden is distributed to whoever built the cage — not only to the forced actor. The constrained actor bears trauma; the cage-builders bear primary moral weight. Anger at the system is accurate moral accounting, not rationalization. Systemic responsibility reduces but does not eliminate individual obligation.",
      "notes": "Connects to FR-4 (neoliberal systems engineer constraints by design) and FR-2 (cowardice builds cages through deferral). Nuances DB-1: information environment architects share responsibility for enabler choices. Source: Thomas review 2026-03-06, trolley problem critique."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-15",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Harm Assessment Framework",
      "text": "Harm requires three parameters: (1) Time window — less than one generation: negligent; 1-2 generations: Thomas's practical obligation horizon; beyond 2 generations: prediction too unreliable to obligate specifically. (2) Scale — micro (individual) and macro (systemic) both morally relevant. (3) Sampling/evidence — grounded claims, not assertion; beware cherry-picked windows. Commitment: reach as far as practically useful, challenge yourself, do your best.",
      "notes": "Operationalizes FP-6 (scope of harm) and FR-2 (temporal distortion). Climate (BS-1) fails all three parameters of harm denial. Source: Thomas review 2026-03-06."
    },
    {
      "id": "DB-14",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Advocacy Is Part of Singer Compliance",
      "status": "retired",
      "retired_note": "ABSORBED INTO FP-5 compliance note (v1.4). Content preserved in FP-5 'Compliance note' section of the .md source.",
      "text": "Meeting FP-5 is not only a personal practice requirement — it includes actively propagating the principle to others. Giving Singer's books, engaging people, modeling the behavior, being recognized as an agent of change: these are compliance, not supererogatory extras. If advocacy can prevent harm without sacrificing anything of moral significance, FP-5 applies to it.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-5, FP-3, FP-8. Resolves demandingness gap from v1.2. Source: Thomas review 2026-03-06."
    },
    {
      "id": "DB-15",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Autonomy Respect Scales with Demonstrated Good Faith",
      "text": "The Kantian obligation to respect interlocutor autonomy is not unconditional. It scales inversely with: (1) demonstrated bad faith — repeated non-truth-seeking pattern; (2) harm enabled — positions represent danger to others (useful idiot function); (3) exhaustion of genuine engagement — exceptional good-faith effort made and documented. When all three conditions are met, goal shifts from autonomy preservation to structural defense and harm prevention.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-3 (conditional), FP-4, FP-8, FR-9, FR-12. Friend S: all three conditions met. Consequentialist override of Kantian constraint — Thomas accepts this tradeoff explicitly. Source: Thomas review 2026-03-06."
    },

    // ── NEW NODES v1.4–v1.8 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    {
      "id": "FP-15",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "FirstPrinciple",
      "label": "Silence Is a Position",
      "text": "Silence under an authoritarian system is not neutrality — it is a categorized historical position. The Flowchart of Historical Accountability (T-11) demonstrates this structurally: every path from 'aware of authoritarian system → yes' leads to a named node. There is no exit from the map. Awareness plus silence = a position. Thomas's obligation to name this (FP-8) is not dependent on efficacy — it is dependent on awareness.",
      "notes": "Links to: FP-8, T-10, T-11, FR-18, DB-1. Historical anchors: post-WWII Germans judged for silence regardless of outcome; Switzerland — committed strategic withdrawer — broke silence in 2026 when continued withdrawal became structurally untenable. Source: dog-walk session, 2026-03-14. v2.0 amendment: Two complementary framings of why silence is consequential. (1) Accountability frame (original): silence gets categorized on the historical map whether intended or not — the witness who stays silent is counted. (2) Market-failure frame (added v2.0): silence is an active mechanism depleting truth supply. In systems where denialism is incentivized and silence is cheaper than speaking, truth becomes functionally scarce — not because truth is objectively rare, but because the supply chain (witnesses bearing record, institutions reporting facts, culture valuing accuracy) has been disrupted. Both framings are true and complementary. The accountability frame explains the speaker's obligation; the market-failure frame explains the systemic consequence of that silence at scale. See new FR-20 (Truth as Scarce Commodity)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-16",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Earned Clarity Distortion",
      "text": "Repeated vindication on large-scale threats sharpens pattern recognition — an asset. But it also compresses moral distance between unambiguous cases (Nazi salute on inaugural stage) and contextually ambiguous ones (friend not reading the book). Trauma-sharpened recognition accelerates compression. Risk: ambiguous data assigned the moral weight of the clearest cases. Active mitigants: willingness to walk away, Grey Rock as active discernment, explicit awareness of this risk.",
      "notes": "Calibration test: 'Am I assigning this situation the moral weight it actually carries, or the weight of the clearest case it resembles?' Connects to: FP-8, FR-9, SA-3, SA-5, T-9. Source: Socratic audit session, 2026-03-13."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-17",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Confrontation: Moral Witness vs. Persuasion Function",
      "text": "Confronting denialism serves two distinct functions: (1) Moral witness — naming evil, bearing public record, refusing to normalize. FP-8 mandates this. Morally necessary regardless of efficacy. (2) Persuasion — attempting to shift beliefs of the person confronted. Historically low-yield against useful idiots and conflict-avoidant actors. Confrontation does not convert useful idiots — it makes them defensive. Most effective resisters during authoritarian collapse built trusted networks; they didn't try to convert the unreliable. Friend-circle reduction is historically validated strategy, not only PTSD-driven avoidance.",
      "notes": "Connects to: FP-8, DB-10, T-7, T-9, FR-16. Source: Socratic audit session, 2026-03-13."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-18",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Filter-Premise Detection: Find the Right Chain",
      "text": "Before walking any reasoning chain, ask: does this person hold a filter-premise that pre-rejects the destination regardless of chain clarity? Filter-premises: futility ('nothing we can do'), irrelevance, source distrust, prior conclusion. Walking more clearly won't help against a filter-premise — find a parallel chain to the same destination from premises they already accept. Protocol: (1) identify filter-premise, (2) check if current chain requires agreement with contested premise, (3) find unblocked chain, (4) acknowledge first chain honestly then put it down, (5) walk unblocked chain. Friend N routing: futility filter → use accountability chain (Germans judged for silence regardless of outcome), not efficacy chain → T-11 as shared inquiry ('where do you see yourself?').",
      "notes": "Connects to: FR-5 (logic targets wrong system), T-10, FP-8, FR-17. Source: Dog-walk session, 2026-03-14."
    },
    {
      "id": "FP-16",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "FirstPrinciple",
      "label": "Promise Trauma",
      "text": "Systems built on foundational promises that are never kept produce intergenerational trauma expressed as oscillation between idealism and cynicism. Americans are not irrational deniers — they are rationally traumatized by a broken constitutional promise (equality, rule of law, dignity as birthright). Denialism is, in this frame, learned self-protection: if idealism leads to betrayal, cynicism becomes an adaptive strategy. This does not excuse denialism. It explains it, and changes the moral allocation: the primary blame falls on those who design and maintain the broken promise, not on those who adapted to it.",
      "notes": "Source: PT$D book C-23 (book-graph: Promise Trauma concept). Nuances DB-1: Trump enablers are practicing moral cowardice *within a system that has already taught them that idealism leads to betrayal.* The cowardice is real; the context matters for understanding it. Also nuances FR-14 (distributed responsibility): the architects of the broken promise bear the heaviest moral weight. Added v2.0 based on cross-graph analysis with book KG."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-16",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Cultural Sensitivity ≠ Cultural Relativism",
      "text": "Cultural sensitivity (respecting persons across cultures as persons) is categorically distinct from cultural relativism (refusing to critique harmful cultural patterns because culturally embedded). Thomas holds the former; explicitly rejects the latter. Intolerance of harmful cultural patterns is an obligation (FP-8), not a failure of empathy. The charge of 'cultural insensitivity' is usually a category error — it conflates critique of a pattern with disrespect for the person. Limit: delivery may warrant adjustment even when substance is correct.",
      "notes": "Illustrative cases: Russian friend defending Putin; Turkish friends' conflict-avoidance around Musk Nazi salute. Grounds: FP-4, FP-8, FP-12. Connects to: FR-16, FR-17, T-9. Source: Socratic audit session, 2026-03-13."
    },

