    {
      "id": "DB-25",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Conscription as Electoral Equalizer: The Structural Case for Universal Draft",
      "text": "Two arguments that must not be conflated. (1) The standard opposition (well-grounded): as historically implemented, the draft is a class weapon — the insulated class reliably exits through deferments; FR-1 strips the credibility of insulated advocates; FP-11/FR-2 flag fear-based national-security justifications as manufactured emergency. (2) The structural democratic argument (non-dismissible): a genuinely universal draft (zero deferments, no class exits) is a different moral object. When wars carry mandatory personal cost across all class lines, the voting electorate transforms — wars become politically expensive, uninformed/insulated voting collapses, and the political class acquires genuine obligation (FR-1). This is a FR-23 argument — national skin-in-the-game as collective democratic infrastructure. Cannot be dismissed as futile: implementation failures in past drafts are empirical claims about political will, not logical defeats of the structural case. Potential solutions (non-waivable service, lottery with zero exemptions) are politically difficult, not logically impossible.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-23 (collective skin-in-the-game as democratic infrastructure), FR-4 (class structure: insulation enables foreign policy adventurism), FR-1 (insulated advocates near-zero credibility; universal draft uniquely reduces insulation). Limiting: FR-14 (war architects bear primary moral weight regardless), FP-11 (fear-based draft advocacy fails distortion test; structural argument does not), FR-15 (forced service is rights cost requiring justified benefit). T-10 futility-veto rejected: implementation difficulty is not a logical defeater. Source: Socratic debate session, 2026-03-21."
    },

