    // ── TENSION NODES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Genuine contradictions (require resolution) and apparent tensions (dissolve
    // under FR-13 or domain separation). Full analysis in .md source.
    // NOTE: T-1 through T-8 were absent from prior JSONC versions (gap identified v1.9 graph audit).

    {
      "id": "T-1",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "\"Argue to Understand\" vs. Building a Debate Weapon",
      "status": "resolved",
      "resolution": "good_faith_conditionality",
      "text": "FP-3 says argue to understand. The Socratic Debater is designed to collapse an opponent's argument. Resolution: FP-3 is conditional on good faith (FR-13). Against bad-faith actors, the goal shifts to structural defense. Socratic questions are invitations to examine one's own beliefs — if the opponent's beliefs are internally coherent, the questions produce no collapse. Collapse reveals pre-existing structural failure, not one caused by the questions.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-3 (conditional), FR-13. Resolves to: DB-10, DB-15. Note: T-1 through T-3 are all FR-13 demonstrations."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-2",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "\"Everyone Deserves Basic Respect\" vs. \"I Don't Want You in My Life\"",
      "status": "apparent",
      "resolution": "FR-13_domain_separation",
      "text": "FP-4 grants universal basic respect. Thomas draws hard relational boundaries. Resolution: Respect is the floor — minimum dignity, not engagement, relationship, or validation. Drawing a relational boundary is not a retraction of respect. FP-4 governs treatment of humanity; relationship is a separate choice. Additional ground: continued engagement without acknowledged harm is itself enabling (FP-8). v1.6: Grey Rock withdrawal is historically grounded (authoritarian collapse trusted networks), not only PTSD-driven avoidance.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-4, FP-8, FR-13, FR-17. CRITICAL for SA-6: T-2's relational boundary logic and the Living in Truth political practice (T-11) are complementary — one governs individual relationships, the other the political function of those choices."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-3",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "\"Never Trust an Absolutist\" Is Self-Referentially Absolute",
      "status": "apparent",
      "resolution": "self_aware_irony_FR-13_demonstration",
      "text": "FP-9 says never trust an absolutist. The statement is itself absolute. Resolution: deliberate self-aware irony demonstrating FR-13. The universal form is not the intended meaning. The point is epistemological absolutism (refusing to revise under evidence), not a prohibition on confident well-grounded positions. Prepared debate response: 'What evidence would change your mind about [X]? I can answer that for everything I hold. The absolutist is the one who can't.'",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-9, FR-13. Debate function: a bad-faith attacker who targets the literal extreme is committing a strawman — name it, don't defend the extreme, redirect to actual claim."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-4",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "Stoic Detachment vs. Political/Emotional Intensity",
      "status": "resolved",
      "resolution": "domain_separation",
      "text": "Stoicism (FR-3) prescribes internal detachment. The political positions are emotionally engaged. Resolution: Stoicism governs internal state, not action choices. Marcus Aurelius governed an empire. Cato chose death rather than submit. Stoic engagement means action without being governed by reactive emotion. What Stoicism prohibits is destabilization — not engagement with injustice.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-3. See SA-3 for where this remains a calibration challenge in practice. STRUCTURAL NOTE (v1.9): FR-3 grounds only 2 nodes in the edge graph, raising the deeper question of whether Stoicism is actually operative vs. aspirationally stated. SA-6 directly extends this tension."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-5",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "Singer's Demanding Standard vs. Thomas's Actual Behavior",
      "status": "apparent",
      "resolution": "dissolved_by_facts",
      "text": "FP-5 applied rigorously is extraordinarily demanding. A debater might assume the position is theoretical. Resolution: the attack fails on the facts. Thomas does not eat meat. Does not buy new clothing. Does not travel for pleasure. Donates substantially — to the degree people urge him to do less. This is not a tension; it is a credibility asset. Residual conceptual point: 'without moral sacrifice' requires judgment — sustainable giving is correct application, not compromise.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-5. Prepared response: 'Before you finish — I don't eat meat, I don't buy new clothing, I don't travel for entertainment, I donate until people tell me I'm giving too much. What were you going to say?'"
    },
    {
      "id": "T-6",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "Collective Obligation vs. Anti-Control Stance",
      "status": "resolved",
      "resolution": "domain_separation",
      "text": "Thomas supports collective enforcement (mandates, regulations) but rejects carrots-and-sticks management and has no interest in using fear to control others. Resolution: different domains, different valid mechanisms. Interpersonal leadership: inspiration-based, voluntary, credibility-driven — carrots/sticks corrupt this domain. Institutional governance: rule-based, enforced, democratically legitimated — enforcement necessary because not everyone acts on rational obligation. The anti-control stance is interpersonal; collective obligation stance is institutional.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-5 tensions_with DB-13. Domain separation: interpersonal vs. institutional mechanisms."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-7",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "\"Get Out of the Way\" vs. Singer Obligation",
      "status": "resolved",
      "resolution": "threshold",
      "text": "'Getting out of the way is often the best option' vs. FP-5 (prevent harm if you can) and FP-8 (silence enables). Resolution: FP-8 is a strong default requiring judgment (FR-13). Withdrawal is justified when continued engagement causes net harm — toxic dynamics, ego battles, presence validates harmful frame. It fails the FP-8 test when presence or absence materially affects whether harm propagates to others. Threshold: valid when (a) game objectives are destructive, (b) engagement validates the frame, or (c) cost exceeds plausible benefit.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-5, FP-8 tensions_with DB-10. Connects to T-9 (moral witness vs. persuasion function) and FR-17."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-8",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "\"Do Not Lie\" vs. Regular Lying in Practice",
      "status": "partially_resolved",
      "resolution": "consequentialist_exception_acknowledged",
      "text": "FP-9 and Mind Map §3 state 'do not lie' as a near-principle. Thomas acknowledges lying regularly when it appears to be the best available option. He dislikes it. He does it. Resolution: 'do not lie' is the strong default (FR-13). Lying is a consequentialist exception — justified when harm of truth exceeds harm of deception in a specific case. This is not hypocrisy: holding honesty as a strong default while acknowledging contextual exceptions is different from claiming never to lie while lying. Risk: 'appears to be the best option' is subjective and can rationalize.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-9. See SA-4 for honest self-audit of this. FR-13: the exception must clear a bar — lying must genuinely appear to produce less harm than truth."
    },

    {
      "id": "T-9",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "Complicity vs. Efficacy in Confrontation",
      "status": "resolved",
      "resolution": "function_separation",
      "text": "FP-8 mandates confronting denialism. Historical record shows confronting useful idiots produces minimal belief change. Resolution: the moral witness function (FP-8-mandated, non-negotiable) is categorically distinct from the persuasion function (cost-benefit governed). Confrontation for moral witness is justified regardless of efficacy. Persuasion requires its own justification. Honest answer: 'Both — and they are not the same justification.'",
      "notes": "Connects to: FP-8, FR-16, FR-17, DB-10, T-7, SA-5. Source: Socratic audit session, 2026-03-13."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-10",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "The Futility Objection: 'Nothing I Can Do' vs. FP-8",
      "status": "resolved",
      "resolution": "accountability_efficacy_distinction",
      "text": "FP-8 mandates speaking out. Futility objection: individual action doesn't change macro outcomes, so there's no point. Resolution: futility targets efficacy; FP-8 grounds accountability. We judge 1940s Germans for the choice to stay silent, not for failing to stop the Holocaust. Moral accountability is independent of causal efficacy. Debate protocol: 'That question is about efficacy. I'm asking about accountability. Do you think German citizens who stayed silent bore moral responsibility even if their speaking wouldn't have changed the outcome?' Live case (2026): Switzerland — most committed strategic withdrawer in history — broke 200 years of silence to condemn U.S. violation of international law. Node 4b (Strategic Withdrawer) is not a stable long-term position; it collapses under sufficient systemic pressure.",
      "notes": "Connects to: FP-8, FP-15, T-9, FR-17, FR-18, SA-5. Source: Dog-walk session, 2026-03-14."
    },
    {
      "id": "T-11",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "The Flowchart of Historical Accountability (Resistance Typology)",
      "status": "tool",
      "text": "Typology mapping individual responses under authoritarian systems. Nodes: 1-Unwitting Enabler (Bonhoeffer's 'stupidity'), 2-Apathetic Conformist, 3-Survival-Driven Complier, 4a-Subtle Agent of Truth (Havel's 'living in truth'), 4b-Strategic Withdrawer (Epicureanism/ataraxia), 5-Deliberately Destructive, 6-Pragmatic Enabler, 7-True Believer, 8-Principled Catalyst, 9-Uncalibrated Idealist, 10-Institutional Defender. Use: show full map, ask interlocutor to self-locate. Do NOT walk them to a conclusion — they will defend the label. Then ask: 'Do you think the people in those nodes escaped what came after?' Switzerland 2026: even Node 4b eventually becomes untenable. All nodes experienced consequences; history did not sort by intended position.",
      "notes": "Links to: FP-8, FP-15, T-10, FR-18, DB-1. Source: Dog-walk session, 2026-03-14; PT$D book historical resistance analysis. CROSS-GRAPH NOTE (v2.5): A prior version cited 'PT$D book BTN-11' as the source for the resistance typology / Node 4a (Living in Truth). This citation was incorrect. MASTER BTN-11 is 'Determined Past vs. Possible Future' (ChunkA: the book's central ambivalence about determinism vs. agency). The resistance typology and Havel's Living in Truth concept in the book are represented by: C-63 (Living in Truth), C-81 (Living in Truth, Havel Full), A-38 (Havel's greengrocer as macro-political act), and BFR-25 (Havel's Living in Truth Framework). The Thomas-graph T-11 node is a distinct, separately developed tool; it draws conceptual inspiration from Havel (book-graph C-63/C-81/BFR-25) but is not a citation of book BTN-11. LIVING IN TRUTH (v1.9): Node 4a names Havel's framework but this KG has used it primarily to classify others. Deeper implication: Thomas is already practicing Living in Truth — his confrontations, friend-circle reductions, and refusal to perform normalcy are political acts (not merely relational ones) in Havel's sense. Relational costs are historically documented as the price of this practice. See full .md entry."
    },

    // ── SELF-AUDIT NODES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Calibration challenges — honest self-assessment where principles meet
    // imperfect practice. Full analysis in .md source.
    // NOTE: SA-1 through SA-4 were absent from JSONC versions prior to v1.9 (gap identified by comprehensive audit).

    {
      "id": "SA-1",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "status": "retired",
      "label": "Singer Compliance Gap [RETRACTED v1.1]",
      "text": "RETRACTED. Original claim: Thomas's failure to give away sufficient income to meet Singer's standard was a genuine compliance gap. Factually wrong — advocacy (giving books, conversations, time) counts as FP-5 compliance under Singer's own logic. See T-5 and DB-14 (retired). Retained for edge-reference integrity.",
      "notes": "Retired v1.1. See T-5 (dissolved by facts + FP-5). No active edges."
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-2",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Patience Threshold Not Fully Principled",
      "text": "FP-3 prescribes arguing to understand; FP-8 mandates engagement. But the threshold between principled withdrawal and convenient avoidance is not fully worked out. Thomas has likely chosen avoidance when engagement was the correct obligation. Honest position: 'The line between principled withdrawal and comfortable avoidance is genuinely difficult. I don't always get it right. The test I try to apply: would my absence materially enable harm to others?'",
      "notes": "FR-13 note: calibration challenge, not absolute failure. Connects to: FP-3, FP-8, DB-10, T-7."
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-3",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Emotional Intensity in Political Positions",
      "text": "Stoicism (FR-3) prescribes not reacting in real-time. Some political positions are written with emotional intensity suggesting reactivity rather than processed, deliberate commitment. Honest position: 'The charge is real and I believe the positions are correct. But stating them from a fully grounded, non-reactive posture is an ongoing discipline — not an accomplished fact.' The target is not zero emotional engagement — it is not being governed by emotion.",
      "notes": "v1.9: FP-11 (fear as distortion) newly routed inward — Thomas's intensity may have a fear component (fear of the stakes being real, of not being heard, of the moment passing). Connects to: FR-3, T-4, SA-5, SA-6, FP-11."
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-4",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Lying as Consequentialist Exception",
      "text": "Thomas holds honesty as a strong default (FP-9) and departs from it consequentially when the harm of truth appears to exceed the harm of deception. He dislikes this. He does it. Honest position: 'I hold honesty as a strong default and depart from it consequentially when the harm of truth appears to exceed the harm of deception. I don't like this. The risk is that \"appears to be the best option\" is a subjective judgment that can rationalize. I try to hold the bar high, but I cannot claim I always do.'",
      "notes": "See T-8 for structural analysis. FR-13: the exception must clear a bar. Risk of rationalization acknowledged. Added v1.3."
    },

    {
      "id": "SA-5",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Presence as Epistemic Variable",
      "text": "Thomas's intensity, accumulated stakes, PTSD-sharpened pattern recognition, and active thesis may cause people to perform their worst epistemics specifically in his presence. Does not exonerate observed patterns — they are real and documented. It is a data-quality variable when reading what friend behavior reveals about those friends. Calibration test: 'Is this who they are, or is this who they become around the weight I carry into this room?' Answer may still be: both — and the thesis still holds. But the question sharpens perception.",
      "notes": "Connects to: FR-9, FR-16, SA-3, T-9. Source: Socratic audit session, 2026-03-13."
    },

    // ── NEW NODES v1.9 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    {
      "id": "FR-19",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Negative Capability",
      "text": "Keats (1817): 'capable of being in uncertainties, mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact and reason.' The active psychological capacity to hold contradictions, ambiguities, and unresolved tensions without collapsing them into false certainty. Not skepticism, passivity, or relativism. Distinct from FP-9 (epistemological humility about revising positions): Negative Capability addresses the relationship to the gaps *between* positions — the unresolved space itself. Counteracts FR-16 (Earned Clarity Distortion): prevents ambiguous cases from being prematurely assigned the moral weight of unambiguous ones. Prerequisite for genuine internal coherence (FP-10 extension): you cannot have a coherent internal scorecard while treating all unresolved tensions as failures.",
      "notes": "STRUCTURAL FINDING (v1.9): FP-9 (curiosity/openness) currently grounds almost nothing in the edge graph. FR-19 is the missing operationalization — the specific practice that makes FP-9's epistemological stance livable. Connects to: FP-9, FP-10, FR-16, SA-3, SA-6. Source: KG cross-analysis session, 2026-03-14."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-20",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Truth as Scarce Commodity",
      "text": "Truth is not merely an epistemic good held by individuals — it is a commodity with a supply chain that can be corrupted at scale. When denialism is systematically incentivized and silence is cheaper than speaking, truth becomes *functionally* scarce. Not because truth is objectively rare, but because the infrastructure for truth-circulation (witnesses bearing record, institutions reporting facts, cultures that value accuracy, media that rewards it) has been deliberately degraded. Thomas's individual commitment to truth (FP-9) must be understood within this systemic context: the cost of individual truth-telling is artificially elevated by the degraded supply chain. Individual honesty is necessary but insufficient — what is required is restoration of the supply chain itself.",
      "notes": "Source: PT$D book title framing ('Truth as Currency in a Market of Denial') and C-node analysis. Connects FP-9 (individual truth-seeking) and FP-15 (silence as market failure) to the systemic level. Grounds the book project itself as a supply-chain intervention — not just Thomas telling the truth, but building infrastructure for truth to circulate. Added v2.0 based on cross-graph analysis."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-21",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Living in Truth as Political Practice",
      "text": "Authenticity under authoritarian or semi-authoritarian conditions is not merely a psychological health choice — it is a *political act* in Havel's sense. Refusing to perform normalcy, preserving authentic community, naming things accurately in public: these constitute the infrastructure through which organized resistance remains possible when conditions shift. The distinction from the merely relational version: in the relational frame, Living in Truth is about personal integrity and boundary-setting. In the political frame, it is about maintaining the social fabric through which collective action becomes possible. Thomas's confrontations, friend-circle reductions, and insistence on accuracy are political acts in this sense, not merely psychological ones.",
      "notes": "Source: PT$D book nodes C-63 (Living in Truth), C-81 (Living in Truth, Havel Full), A-38 (Havel's greengrocer as macro-political act), BFR-25 (Havel's Living in Truth Framework). CORRECTION (v2.5): Prior version cited 'PT$D book BTN-11 (Resistance Typology Flowchart, Node 4a)' — this was incorrect. MASTER BTN-11 is 'Determined Past vs. Possible Future', not the resistance typology. The relevant book-graph nodes for this concept are C-63, C-81, A-38, and BFR-25 as listed above. Elevated from a note in T-11 (v1.9) to a full framework node in v2.0. The elevation matters: naming it as a framework-level commitment anchors Thomas's personal actions (the book, the debates, the friend-circle choices) in a recognized historical resistance strategy, giving them political weight beyond the psychological. Connects to T-11, FR-17 (moral witness), FP-8 (obligation to name harmful patterns)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-22",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Cultural Pathology Patterns",
      "text": "Denialism is not only a product of individual fear or ego distortion — it is often the output of a *cultural operating system* that systematically teaches its members to deny, deflect, or rationalize harmful patterns. Cultures transmit epistemic pathologies the way they transmit values: through socialization, status incentives, and collective trauma. A Russian friend's Putin apologetics, a Turkish friend's conflict-avoidance, an American friend's Trump normalization — these are not only individual moral failures. They are the predictable outputs of cultural inheritance systems that have learned to make certain lies easier to believe. This framework does not excuse individual positions. It explains their stubbornness and changes the diagnostic: you are not arguing with a person, you are arguing with a person *plus* their cultural operating system. FR-22 supplements FR-9 (ego distortion) and FR-16 (earned clarity distortion) by adding the cultural transmission layer.",
      "notes": "Source: PT$D book CS-nodes (Cultural/Systemic Distortion Patterns). Added v2.0 based on cross-graph analysis. Required by DB-16 (cultural sensitivity ≠ relativism): Thomas can critique cultural patterns while respecting persons precisely because FR-22 distinguishes the cultural system from the individual. Connects to FP-16 (Promise Trauma as the American version of cultural epistemic inheritance), FR-9 (ego distortion as individual layer), FR-16 (earned clarity risk when applying cultural critique)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-23",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Collective Solidarity vs. Individual Obligation",
      "text": "Individual compliance with Singer's standard (FP-5: give to the point of marginal utility, practice advocacy) is necessary but not sufficient. Collective action — organized solidarity, mutual aid, shared infrastructure for truth-telling — is *different in kind* from aggregated individual compliance. The distinction: individual obligation asks what you owe as a person. Collective solidarity asks what infrastructure the system requires before individual action becomes effective. Thomas's personal practice (giving, writing the book, modeling behavior) satisfies individual obligation. But the book itself, the Socratic Debater, and the act of preserving authentic communities (FR-21) are contributions to the collective infrastructure layer — they create conditions under which others' individual actions become more effective. This framework prevents the error of thinking that maximum individual compliance is sufficient, and names the organizational/cultural layer as a distinct moral domain.",
      "notes": "Source: PT$D book A-nodes (Activism as Systemic Truth Currency). Added v2.0 based on cross-graph analysis. Complements FP-5 (Singer standard) by identifying what personal obligation cannot cover. Connects to FR-21 (Living in Truth as political practice builds the collective layer) and T-6 (collective obligation vs. anti-control principle). The key distinction from T-6: T-6 addresses the tension between collective obligation and individual autonomy; FR-23 addresses the distinction between individual compliance and collective capacity as different modes of moral action."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-24",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Functional Discourse",
      "text": "Functional discourse is an exchange in which both parties share epistemic standards, argue in good faith toward a conclusion neither has predetermined, and accept that updating their position on evidence is not a defeat. Four minimum conditions: (1) Shared referents — words mean the same thing to both parties; failure mode: semantic drift. (2) Falsifiability — claims can in principle be proven wrong; failure mode: unfalsifiable claims. (3) Update willingness — new evidence can change stated positions; failure mode: conclusion-first reasoning. (4) Identity-position separation — being wrong does not threaten the person's self-concept; failure mode: position fused with identity. Condition 4 is the load-bearing one: when identity and position fuse, conditions 1–3 become inert. This is not stupidity (see FR-25); it is the structural effect of political systems that weaponize identity as coalition-binding material. The deliberate firehose of contradiction is discourse sabotage — it exploits Brandolini's Law by generating contradictions faster than analysis can process them; the goal is not persuasion but exhaustion of the analytical frame. Against a discourse-broken actor, refutation becomes performance for the audience — useful, but categorically different from functional exchange. The drapedapes protocol (One Claim / One Response; Good-Faith Only; Know When to Exit) is a resource-conservation strategy for when FR-24 conditions are not met, not a description of functional discourse itself.",
      "notes": "Added v2.8. Source: Thomas / Claude conversation 2026-03-23; James O'Brien LBC segment, Strait of Hormuz analysis (2026-03-23); drapedapes.org discourse framework (2025-02-05); ChatGPT/Gemini political analysis sessions (2026-03-23). Connects to: FP-8 (discourse collapse makes silence enabling), FR-2 (temporal distortion exploited by discourse-broken tactics), FR-20 (truth scarcity is what discourse breakdown produces at scale), FR-25 (load-bearing: which failure type determines whether FR-24 conditions can be restored), DB-1 (discourse failure explains why moral cowardice resists argument). Load-bearing edge in: FR-25 → FR-24 (grounds, LB); FR-24 → DB-1 (grounds, LB)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FR-25",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Taxonomy of Capacity Failure",
      "text": "Not all failures of discourse participation are equivalent. Three types must be distinguished: (1) Capacity-limited — genuine cognitive ceiling; cannot follow complex argument chains regardless of incentive or information environment; discourse not fixable; low moral allocation. (2) Motivated reasoner — has capacity; deploys it backward from a predetermined identity or incentive conclusion; discourse possibly fixable under narrow conditions; high moral allocation — this is cowardice or corruption. (3) Epistemically vulnerable — has capacity; operating in a corrupted information environment; trusts wrong sources; most addressable by discourse; medium moral allocation shared between individual and system designers. FR-2's cowardice reframe ('they won't look' not 'they can't see') applies specifically to motivated reasoners. 'Stupid' used precisely names the capacity-limited type; the word fails when applied indiscriminately — and resistance to the word is itself epistemic avoidance that forces all failures into the motivational frame, flattering the capacity-limited while excusing the motivated reasoner. Trump's coalition contains all three types simultaneously: the movement is engineered to function across them — simple repetition for the capacity-limited; permission structure for the motivated reasoners; information environment saturation for the epistemically vulnerable. This is a multi-layer communication architecture, not exploitation of stupidity.",
      "notes": "Added v2.8. Source: Thomas / Claude conversation 2026-03-23; Haidt, The Righteous Mind; Bonhoeffer, 'On Stupidity' (1943); Cipolla, The Basic Laws of Human Stupidity (1976). Connects to: FR-24 (load-bearing: which type determines whether functional discourse is available), FR-2 (cowardice reframe is type-specific), FP-4 (apparent tension with respect principle; resolution: respect is a floor, not an accuracy suppressor), DB-1 (moral allocation for Trump enablement depends on which type they are), SA-4 (Thomas must allocate failure type accurately without using taxonomy as rationalization for contempt)."
    },

    {
      "id": "FR-26",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Isometric Hold: The Hidden Cost of Equilibrium",
      "text": "A system maintaining apparent equilibrium with zero displacement is not doing zero work — it is performing an active, energy-intensive hold against ongoing opposing forces. The snapshot frame (W = Fd, d = 0) is myopic: it ignores the metabolic/energy cost of maintaining the static state. Physics: during isometric muscle contraction, ATP burns at full rate despite zero external movement; the control system must increase gain to maintain the hold, which raises feedback delay relative to gain, producing limit-cycle oscillations ('shaking') before structural failure. Social/political analog: the status quo is an isometric hold. Massive expenditures of social ATP (enforcement, propaganda, legal cycles, manufactured emergency) are consumed to keep displacement at zero against suppressed dissent, structural change, or entropy. The stability is expensive and concealed by the snapshot frame. Failure progression: (1) hold cost increases → gain compensates; (2) feedback delay grows; (3) limit cycles emerge — civil volatility, unrest, the 'shaking'; (4) component snap — institutional collapse, abrupt regime change. Diagnostic implication: the most dangerous systems are those performing the most expensive holds, not those with the most visible turbulence. Absence of visible movement is not evidence of stability. Application to FR-2: 'Extend and Pretend' institutional behavior is isometric — costs accumulate while the visible state appears unchanged. Application to political systems: authoritarian stability reads as acceptance; it is more accurately an isometric hold — the population exerts constant suppressed force; the regime expends constant energy to prevent displacement; collapse appears sudden only to snapshot readers.",
      "notes": "Added v2.9. Source: Thomas / Gemini conversation 2026-03-23: _CRE Market Faces Debt Maturity Wall.md; controls engineering applied to social dynamics. Grounds: FP-8 (silence is an active hold, not neutrality) + FR-4 (hold cost extracted from those at the bottom) + FR-2 (snapshot fallacy). Connects to: FR-2 (rates/levels; extend-and-pretend), FR-4 (cost extraction), FP-8 (silence as maintenance), DB-5 (time/energy extracted from those with fewest reserves), FR-25 (hold costs degrade capacity), EV-10 (CRE data as instantiation)."
    },

    {
      "id": "FR-28",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "Framework",
      "label": "Chain-Span Exploitation as Systemic Architecture",
      "text": "The system is designed such that it exploits chain-span limitations. Structurally distinct from 'designed to exploit' — the distinction matters for moral allocation (FR-14). The dominant political and economic communication architectures are optimized for chain-span-limited reasoning: simple slogans, transactional framing, zero-sum games, emotional salience over structural analysis. These fit within 2-3 links. Counter-arguments (cooperation enlarges the total, money is not value, accountability serves long-term interest) require 5-7 links. The architecture doesn't need conscious design to exploit; it needs only to be selected for by incentives that reward simplicity and punish complexity. Three actor types: (1) Nefarious actors who deliberately design for chain-span exploitation — primary moral weight; (2) Leaders who accept the architecture without weaponizing it — intermediate moral weight; (3) Participants who cannot see the architecture — low individual moral weight, high systemic consequence. The money-worship instantiation: the chain from money to what actually constitutes value has too many links for someone at a 3-link ceiling. Zero-sum framing is the default game-theoretic model for chain-span-limited reasoning; cooperative outcomes require modeling second-order effects that exceed the ceiling.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-27 (cognitive mechanism), FR-4 (political economy), FR-8 (algorithmic amplification), FR-25 (individual taxonomy), FR-14 (moral allocation across actor types). Connects to: DB-29, FR-9, FR-20, SA-9. Source: Thomas / Claude session 2026-03-25; Pritzker commencement speech. Added v3.4 (2026-03-25)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-17",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "The Book as Witness Record, Not Persuasion Project",
      "text": "PT$D's function is the moral witness function — a framework, a record, and a line in the sand. Success criterion: does the record exist? Has the pattern been named correctly? These questions are independent of audience size or commercial success. Thomas does not have the financial or emotional resources to push the book into market success — forcing it at that cost is self-defeating (FP-5 sustainability). The book will find its readers or it won't. The record exists either way.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-8, FR-17 (load-bearing), FR-20, FR-21, FP-5 (Maslow/sustainability). Connects to: SA-7 (capacity constraint). Source: Thomas's stated position, 2026-03-15; 20260215 Friend S USA said i will visit.md. Added v2.2."
    },

    {
      "id": "SA-7",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Capacity Constraint: cPTSD, Resource Depletion, and the Requirement to Be Safe",
      "text": "Thomas has severe, diagnosed cPTSD with documented physiological consequences (night terrors, vomiting loops, sleep disruption) and is caretaking a homeless, mentally ill parent under material and emotional scarcity. He does not have the financial or emotional resources for overt activism. The principled case: FR-11 (Maslow domain) — obligations scale with capability and domain position; historian-informed survival strategy (FR-17, T-11 Node 4a) — effective resisters used quiet discernment and network preservation, not overt confrontation. Self-audit: the constraint is real but can rationalize beyond its actual limit. The honest position: the book and authentic-network preservation are what is available; overt activism is not, without causing further harm. This is Node 4a (Subtle Agent of Truth) at available capacity — not Node 4b (Strategic Withdrawer).",
      "notes": "Connects to: FR-11 (load-bearing), FP-5, FR-3, SA-3, SA-6, T-11. Source: 20260215 Friend S USA said i will visit.md; Thomas's stated position, 2026-03-15. Added v2.2."
    },

    {
      "id": "SA-8",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Cognitive Synthesis Self-Model: Process + Trauma + AI",
      "text": "Thomas explicitly identifies and affirms the architecture generating his analytical synthesis — not as a hardware gift (IQ) but as a process triad: (1) System 2 as engine — deliberate, high-energy, slow-burn cognitive integration; explicit modeling of feedback delays, transfer functions, and non-linearities across domains; cognitively expensive; Thomas runs this at high frequency and intensity on structural problems while most people default to System 1. (2) Emotional trauma as catalyst — severe early-onset unpredictability (cPTSD, see SA-7) forced survival-grade sensitivity to initial conditions, feedback dynamics, and state transitions; the internal model built for detecting environmental threat transfers directly to analytical work on complex adaptive systems. (3) AI as cognitive exoskeleton — handles granular detail and formal argument rendering, preventing buffer overflow and allowing Thomas to maintain focus on high-level frameworks. Key calibration: Thomas explicitly rejects the IQ-hardware framing — the synthesis is process-dependent, not innately genetic, which means (a) it is teachable and transferable, (b) it degrades under resource depletion (SA-7 disrupts all three legs), (c) it compounds with use. Honest tension with SA-7: SA-7 maps the constraint face of the underlying conditions (trauma → cPTSD → capacity limit); SA-8 maps the constructive face (trauma → hyper-observation → analytical depth). Both are simultaneously true. Thomas cannot invoke SA-8 to cancel SA-7, nor SA-7 to dismiss SA-8. The question live in SA-6: does the output come from the witness obligation or from ego investment in having seen it first?",
      "notes": "Added v2.9. Source: Thomas / Gemini conversation 2026-03-23: _CRE Market Faces Debt Maturity Wall.md; Thomas's explicit self-identification during calibration exchange; Kahneman, Thinking Fast and Slow. Connects to: SA-7 (constraint face of same conditions; tensions_with), FR-5 (System 2 neuroscience; EV-8), FR-19 (Negative Capability required to hold uncertainty without forced closure), DB-17 (book as product and output of the process), T-4 (Stoic detachment vs. intensity: trauma catalyst drives the intensity SA-3 and SA-6 flag), FR-26 (same systems-thinking lens Thomas applies externally)."
    },

    {
      "id": "SA-6",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "SelfAudit",
      "label": "Outcome Attachment: Relational vs. Historical",
      "text": "The book's Stoic framework requires detachment from outcome, not just emotional reactivity. Question: is Thomas attached to outcomes in ways that compromise Stoic integrity (FR-3, T-4)? Two registers: (1) Relational outcome attachment — needing this specific person, in this conversation, to change their mind. Stoic test: after a confrontation that produced no belief change, what is the residual feeling? 'I said what needed to be said; the record exists' = detachment. Frustration, urgency to re-engage = attachment. (2) Historical/macro attachment — urgency about whether the moment goes the right direction. Not obviously ego investment; urgency proportionate to threat (T-10, T-11) is not attachment. The question is whether some urgency comes from needing to be seen or vindicated vs. from accurate threat assessment. Honest position: uncertain. Active monitoring required.",
      "notes": "STRUCTURAL FINDING (v1.9): FR-3 (Stoicism) grounds only 2 nodes in the edge graph — Stoicism is nearly structurally inert. SA-6 raises the deeper question: is Stoicism actually shaping behavior in traceable ways, or is it primarily a stated value? FP-11 (fear as distortion) has never been routed to Thomas's own SA nodes — that gap is part of SA-6's context. Connects to: FR-3, T-4, SA-3, SA-5, FR-16, FR-17, FP-11. Source: KG cross-analysis session, 2026-03-14."
    },

    {
      "id": "T-12",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "TensionNode",
      "label": "Perpetual Openness vs. Integration Requirement",
      "text": "FP-9 requires perpetual openness to revision — no position is immune to evidence. FP-10 requires internal coherence — beliefs, statements, and actions must integrate. Apparent tension: you cannot simultaneously treat all positions as fully provisional (FP-9) and require that they integrate coherently (FP-10). If everything is always open to revision, nothing can be integrated; if everything is integrated, revision becomes systemically disruptive. Resolution: FP-9 and FP-10 operate at different temporal levels and different epistemic objects. FP-9 governs the update protocol — how Thomas responds to new evidence. FP-10 governs the current-state requirement — that what Thomas currently holds must be internally consistent. A person can be maximally open to revision while requiring that their current snapshot of beliefs integrate coherently. The tension resolves when FP-9 is understood as applying to the revision moment and FP-10 as applying to the holding state. FR-19 (Negative Capability) provides the psychological capacity to hold both without collapse. Residual vulnerability: rapid belief updating temporarily disrupts integration — the speed-of-revision case is the live risk.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-9 (perpetual openness), FP-10 (integration requirement). Resolution mechanism: FR-19 (Negative Capability). Status: Apparently resolved by the temporal distinction; residual speed-of-revision vulnerability. Added v2.4 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-18",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Reconciliation Requires Architectural Change, Not Time",
      "text": "Re-engagement after a principled exit is conditional on demonstrated epistemic restructuring — not time elapsed, emotional readiness, or surface apology. A valid apology must contain evidence of the changed mechanism, not only regret for the outcome. 'Commitment to prevention' requires that the original harm become structurally impossible to repeat, not merely that the person intends differently. Architectural change is retrospectively verifiable only: the condition cannot be satisfied at the moment of apology — only by observed behavior over time. This is accurate epistemology applied to trust, not cruelty.",
      "notes": "Pairs with DB-10 (licenses exit) and DB-15 (good faith requirement): DB-18 governs the conditions for return. Connects to T-2 (relational boundary resolution — DB-18 specifies what crossing back requires). SA-6 note: the conditional door is not outcome attachment — it is a structural requirement independent of emotional state. Grounds: FR-17 (load-bearing), DB-10, DB-15, FP-3. Added v2.3 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-19",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Climate Change Is Real, Urgent, and the FMEA of Denial Is Unacceptable",
      "text": "Climate change is real and anthropogenic. The debate is about the level of urgency, not reality — but the FMEA (failure mode and effects analysis) of denial makes delay structurally unacceptable: the cost of under-responding to a real crisis vastly outweighs the cost of over-responding. Data is unambiguous; manufactured doubt has corrupted the public discussion, not the scientific record. The asymmetry of harm (FR-15: scale is civilizational, time window is 1–2 generations, evidence is robust) licenses strong conclusions. Over-responding to climate reality is not a significant concern — the downside of aggressive action on a real problem is manageable; the downside of inaction is catastrophic. Declares BS-1.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-15 (harm assessment), FR-22 (manufactured denial as cultural pathology), FP-8 (obligation to name harmful patterns), FR-20 (manufactured doubt depletes truth supply). Declares BS-1. Added v2.4 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-20",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Mass Incarceration Is Structural Failure; Rehabilitation Is the Obligation",
      "text": "Mass incarceration in the US is the continuation of racialized labor control by other means — a structural feature of the post-1970s neoliberal system, not a natural consequence of crime rates. Society's obligation is rehabilitation, not punishment. Prison as currently practiced produces recidivism, trauma, and dependency. The failure is one of imagination and political economy: rehabilitative systems are conceivable; they are difficult to fund within a system that profits from incarceration. Only in rare cases does someone pose so irreducible a threat that permanent removal is warranted — and even then, the rehabilitation obligation persists. The death penalty is rejected on these grounds. Declares BS-2.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-4 (mass incarceration as neoliberal labor control), FR-14 (distributed responsibility: cage-builders bear primary weight), FP-4 (dignity grounds rehabilitation obligation), FP-12 (obligation extends to all conscious beings). Declares BS-2. Added v2.4 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-21",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Immigration Is a Net Benefit; Restriction Is Fear-Based Culture War",
      "text": "Available data does not support the claim that immigration damages the United States — the preponderance of evidence runs the other direction. America's foundational self-image (Statue of Liberty) reflects what the data shows: an immigrant nation is stronger, more innovative, more resilient. Immigration reform is needed to improve the process, reduce backlogs, and create humane pathways — not to reduce immigration. The restriction frame is manufactured: a fear/ignorance-based culture war topic deployed to divide people and suppress class solidarity. FR-4 identifies the structural motive: labor that cannot move freely is labor that can be controlled. Declares BS-3.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-14 (immigrants face constrained choices — restriction is cage-building), FR-15 (harm assessment: data shows net benefit, not harm), FR-22 (immigration restriction as fear-based cultural pathology), FP-12 (all conscious beings regardless of national origin). Declares BS-3. Added v2.4 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-22",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "AI Requires Human Obligation-Holders; Intelligence Without Obligation Is Not Credible",
      "text": "FP-1 (Obligation Precedes Intelligence) is the direct application: intelligence — even superior intelligence — confers no credibility without accepting consequence. AI systems cannot accept consequence; they cannot bear obligation. Therefore: AI-generated content requires human adoption to be credible; AI governance must center obligation-holders, not capability-holders; the human role in the age of AI is explicitly the obligation role — the thing AI cannot perform. This is not AI fear. It is a structural argument: capability without obligation is the credibility formula's denominator approaching zero. The more capable AI becomes, the more critical it is that human obligation-holders remain in the loop — because capability without obligation scales precisely as a threat, not a safeguard. Declares BS-4.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-1 (Obligation Precedes Intelligence — load-bearing), FR-1 (credibility formula: AI has capability but cannot bear obligation), FP-8 (naming the structural implications is required). Declares BS-4. Added v2.4 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-23",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Education Should Be Free; Current System Perpetuates Inequality and Degrades Quality",
      "text": "Education is a public good, not a market commodity. The current US system functions as an exclusive club: access is determined by wealth, geography, and family, not aptitude or work. This is both a justice failure and a systemic failure: a society paying for declining-quality education while making it economically inaccessible is producing the epistemic degradation it cannot afford. The neoliberal commodification of education (FR-4) is the mechanism — when education is a product, it optimizes for revenue, not for the production of citizens capable of critical thinking. The result: increasing cost, decreasing quality, increasing stratification. Free public education through the highest level the student can benefit from is the policy implication — not as charity, but as the infrastructure investment that makes everything else possible. Declares BS-5.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-8 (democratic architecture requires educated citizens capable of dual-process cognition), FR-23 (collective solidarity requires collective epistemic infrastructure), FR-4 (commodification as mechanism of degradation), FP-5 (Singer: education as a form of systemic obligation). Declares BS-5. Added v2.4 (2026-03-16)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-24",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Harm-Avoidance Institutional Frames Produce the Harm They Prohibit",
      "text": "The 'Do No Harm' frame, when deployed as an institutional operating principle, systematically generates the harm it prohibits. Mechanism: (1) harm-avoidance locates risk in action, not inaction — inaction feels safe but its costs are deferred and diffuse (FR-2 temporal distortion); (2) liability structures amplify this asymmetry — professionals who act bear traceable legal exposure; those who fail to act bear none — cage-builders (FR-14) designed this; (3) economic pressure reduces information per case, increasing uncertainty and thus risk-aversion; time is the missing variable. Domain-general: applies to medicine, law, finance, governance, education — any institutional domain where harm-avoidance is the operative ethical frame. The beneficence reframe inverts the calculus: suffering is the baseline against which action is measured, not the risk against which inaction is declared safe. Requires FR-15 active harm-assessment and FR-14 accurate distribution of moral weight. EV-9 (ACE Study) is the domain evidence: institutional non-adoption of childhood trauma screening is harm-avoidance producing harm.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-5 (beneficence as correct frame — load-bearing), FR-2 (temporal distortion: deferred costs feel safe), FR-14 (liability as cage-building), FR-15 (harm requires active multi-parameter assessment), FP-8 (inaction perpetuates harm). Supports: EV-9. Source: Thomas's essay on 'Do No Harm' vs. 'Reduce Suffering'; session analysis, 2026-03-19. Added v2.6 (2026-03-19)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-29",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "The Indivisible Vote: Moral Package Deal",
      "text": "A vote is morally indivisible — you purchase the entire package. When a voter claims they voted for the best option for their family, the claim requires assessment of the full moral cost: a convicted sexual predator, the most prominent non-Epstein/Maxwell figure in the Epstein files, an administration whose members the voter likely cannot name a single non-compromised member of, and a leader who pitches zero-sum games as governance. If the voter cannot name what moral cost they accepted, they didn't assess it, and the rational family-interest claim is post-hoc rationalization (FR-9: ego distortion). The cheating trap door: once the vote is cast, rational self-interest requires the cheater to win and keep winning — accountability applied to your side now threatens you. This requires abandoning rule of law and honest governance, because those mechanisms applied fairly would indict the team you chose. This is why MAGA supporters must actively defend corruption. Zero-sum framing is the only framework available once accountability has been abandoned: if you cannot win by the rules, your only path is cheating where you are on the cheating side. Every additional day of defense deepens the moral sunk cost.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FP-1 (obligation to assess), FP-8 (silence enables), FR-1 (credibility formula), FR-24 (discourse collapse), FR-25 (capacity taxonomy), DB-1 (moral cowardice — DB-29 adds the trap door that locks it in), FR-9 (ego distortion), FR-8 (abandonment of rule of law), FR-28 (zero-sum as chain-span-limited game theory). Connects to: DB-18, FR-4, DB-30, DB-31. Source: Thomas / Claude session 2026-03-25. Added v3.4 (2026-03-25)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-30",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Temporal Evidence of Moral Failure: The Timeline Is Incriminating",
      "text": "The timing of a political reversal is the moral assessment, not incidental to it. If a Trump supporter claims they were conned, 'When did you change your mind and what changed it?' is incriminating on any timeline. Recent answer: evidence was available for 10+ years from sources with established credibility, provided directly at personal cost — the lateness documents deliberate ignorance. Early-but-unarticulated answer: years of continued defense after the internal shift = moral cowardice in purest form. 'I was conned' answer: the con required active cooperation — ignoring counter-evidence from trusted sources over years is not gullibility, it is FR-9 as a lifestyle. The TDS dismissal is FR-18 (filter-premise) in operation: it pre-rejects the destination before any chain is walked, and converts the critic's vindicated track record into evidence of pathology. This is ego distortion weaponized as social label. For PT$D: the enabler's future reversal does not exonerate — it documents the duration of the enabling. Silence over time is not a gap in the record; it is the record.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-2 (temporal distortion), DB-18 (architectural change requirement deepened by timeline), FP-8 (silence enables), FP-15 (silence is a position), FR-17 (moral witness creates the record), DB-1 (moral cowardice), FR-9 (TDS as ego distortion), FR-18 (TDS as filter-premise). Connects to: DB-29 (trap door deepens yearly), SA-5 (presence as epistemic variable). Source: Thomas / Claude session 2026-03-25. Added v3.4 (2026-03-25)."
    },

    {
      "id": "DB-31",
      "person": "thomas",
      "type": "DerivedBelief",
      "label": "Credibility Destruction Asymmetry",
      "text": "Credibility is destroyed asymmetrically: fast to lose, slow to rebuild, and once lost, genuine belief becomes indistinguishable from tactical positioning. This asymmetry is implicit in FR-1's time-integration but deserves standalone treatment. The indistinguishability problem: when someone who spent years dismissing evidence reverses course, the reversal could be genuine self-correction, tactical self-deprecation to obscure knowingly compromised choices, or social positioning as the cost of the old position exceeds its benefit. The person's credibility — the instrument you would use to assess sincerity — has been destroyed by the same behavior pattern. FR-1 formula: intelligence may be intact, but obligation has atrophied and insulation remains high. At near-zero credibility, signal is indistinguishable from noise. This is why DB-18 requires architectural change, not surface apology: only structural behavioral evidence over time can overcome the indistinguishability problem. Words are cheap and unreadable at low credibility.",
      "notes": "Grounds: FR-1 (credibility formula — time-integration implies asymmetry), FR-2 (time-integrated pattern), DB-18 (architectural change as minimum threshold), DB-30 (timeline as record of destruction), DB-29 (trap door deepens destruction), FP-9 (truth-seeking requires reliable signals), SA-5 (Thomas's perceptive limits), FR-25 (genuine cognitive update vs. indistinguishable type). Source: Thomas / Claude session 2026-03-25. Added v3.4 (2026-03-25)."
    }
  ]
}
